==browser_storage
Browser storage options: Cache, Cookies, Indexed DB, Local Storage, Session Storage.
Non-Persistent: Cache, Session storage.
Persistent: Local Storage, Cookies (user-configurable expiry)
Same-origin Policy(access except from domain setting data prevented): IndexedDB, localStorage, sessionStorage, and cookies
Session Storage survives page reloads.
HTTP-only Cookies: Cannot be read by JavaScript, sent only via HTTP requests.
Web Storage API: Refers to localStorage and sessionStorage.

Comparison Table
Parameter          | Cache Storage    | Cookies           | IndexedDB    | Local Storage | Session Storage
---                | ---              | ---               | ---          | ---           | ---
Capacity           | varying          | 4 KB              | ~50 MB       | 10 MB         | 5 MB
Browser            | HTML5            | HTML5             | HTML5        | HTML5         | HTML5
Accessible from    | Any Window       | Any Window        | Same origin  | Any Window    | Same tab
Expires            | Manually set     | User-configurable | Never        | Never         | On tab close
Storage location   | Browser & Server | Browser & Server  | Browser only | Browser only  | Browser only
Sent with requests | Yes              | Yes               | No           | No            | No
HTTP-only support  | No               | Yes               | No           | No            | No

Network access, Domain, TLS security != Storage access security
* no way to tell user reliably to remove local data from incidents
* connectio success + HTTPS allows HTTP Clear-Site-Data: "storage", "cookies", "cache"
  - ie on logout, indicent, XSS detection, compromise, pw change, account deletion
* cert revocation, ie on key rotation, CA policy dont imply origin was hacked
* no object capability model applied/no cryptographic binding between storage and origin validity

web security fix via object capability model
* Capability-safe JavaScript (or WASM subset) to Enforce unforgeable references.
* Browser-native capability semantics for Storage access via capabilities, not origin-keying.
* Revocation protocol for Standard way to invalidate capabilities (CapTP, GNAP, ZCAP-LD).
* Cryptographic binding for Capabilities tied to agent identity (like Fuchsia does).
